  "answer_text": "Great question! That white film is almost certainly **limescale** (also called **mineral scale** or **hard water deposits**). Here's a breakdown:\n\n---\n\n### What It Is\nThe white film is typically **calcium carbonate** \u2014 a harmless mineral deposit that is especially common in hard water areas. Calcium carbonate is a key component of hard water, which is water that contains high concentrations of minerals like calcium and magnesium.\n\nEven filtered fridge water isn't necessarily free of these minerals. Water filters, particularly those using activated carbon, are designed to remove impurities such as chlorine, sediment, and certain organic compounds \u2014 but they don't always remove dissolved minerals like calcium and magnesium.\n\n---\n\n### How It Forms\nWhen a drinking glass is filled with water, limescale tags along and settles in the glass. Once the water has been used or evaporated, the limescale sticks around, resulting in the glass having a white residue or film. These minerals adhere to glassware surfaces, and once they've precipitated out of the water, they are no longer removable by simple rinsing because they are no longer dissolved.\n\n---\n\n### Is It Dangerous?\nThe white residue is the result of minerals carried through your water and is typically called \"limescale.\" Higher amounts usually signify higher levels of water hardness. Limescale is **harmless to human health**.\n\n---\n\n### How to Clean It Off\n- You can make your own cleaning solution by combining **equal parts vinegar and water**. Submerge the affected glassware in the solution and let it soak overnight for best results, then rinse with warm water until the limescale is gone.\n- You can also use **baking soda or lemon juice** along with a lint-free cloth to rub away limescale.\n- Alternatively, try a **store-bought cleaner** specifically designed to remove mineral deposits from glass surfaces, which may contain acids that effectively remove the buildup.\n\n---\n\n### Quick Test\nIf you ever want to confirm it's limescale and not something else: place a sample of the residue in a cup of vinegar \u2014 if the particles are from hard water, they will **bubble up and dissolve** into the vinegar.\n\nSo in short \u2014 it's a completely natural and harmless result of the minerals naturally present in your water, even after filtering!",
